From: open-nanokvm-pro <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] pwm: dwc: express the two DC extremes instead of -ERANGE

The DesignWare APB timer holds the low period in LD_CNT and the high
period in LD_CNT2, and each is "count + 1" input clock periods long. A
waveform with no high period at all, and one with no low period, are
therefore the two requests the hardware cannot generate, and the driver
returns -ERANGE for both.

Both of those are a DC level, and the timer does have one DC level: the
one it drives while it is stopped. Meet the no-high-period request by
stopping the timer -- the only constant-inactive output this IP has for
a normal-polarity consumer -- and clamp the no-low-period request to the
shortest low period there is, a single tick out of the whole period.

The caller this matters for is pwm-backlight, which asks for exactly
these two states at the ends of its brightness range. It ignores the
apply() return value, so on a board with neither an enable GPIO nor a
regulator -- where pwm_backlight_power_off() keeps the PWM enabled at
duty 0 deliberately, to get a constant inactive output -- a backlight
told to go out simply keeps the duty cycle it already had, and the panel
stays lit with bl_power reading 1. The other end is the same bug in the
other direction: default-brightness-level at max never applies.

diff -u -r a/drivers/pwm/pwm-dwc-core.c b/drivers/pwm/pwm-dwc-core.c
--- a/drivers/pwm/pwm-dwc-core.c	2026-09-12 14:38:47.973711416 -0600
+++ b/drivers/pwm/pwm-dwc-core.c	2026-09-12 14:39:19.288302541 -0600
@@ -60,17 +60,37 @@
 	 * Calculate width of low and high period in terms of input clock
 	 * periods and check are the result within HW limits between 1 and
 	 * 2^32 periods.
+	 *
+	 * Both ends of that range are one whole period long, because a load
+	 * count is "value + 1" input clock periods -- so a waveform with no
+	 * high period at all, and one with no low period, are the two
+	 * requests this timer cannot generate. Both are DC, and the timer has
+	 * exactly one DC level: whatever it drives while it is stopped. Meet
+	 * the no-high-period request by stopping it, and clamp the no-low-
+	 * period one to the shortest low period there is, a single tick out
+	 * of the whole period.
+	 *
+	 * -ERANGE would be worse for every caller. pwm-backlight asks for
+	 * precisely these two states at the ends of its brightness range and
+	 * ignores the error, so a backlight told to go out keeps whatever
+	 * duty cycle it had.
 	 */
-	tmp = DIV_ROUND_CLOSEST_ULL(active, dwc->clk_ns);
-	if (tmp < 1 || tmp > (1ULL << 32))
-		return -ERANGE;
-	low = tmp - 1;
-
 	tmp = DIV_ROUND_CLOSEST_ULL(idle, dwc->clk_ns);
-	if (tmp < 1 || tmp > (1ULL << 32))
+	if (tmp > (1ULL << 32))
 		return -ERANGE;
+	if (tmp < 1) {
+		__dwc_pwm_set_enable(dwc, pwm->hwpwm, false);
+		return 0;
+	}
 	high = tmp - 1;
 
+	tmp = DIV_ROUND_CLOSEST_ULL(active, dwc->clk_ns);
+	if (tmp > (1ULL << 32))
+		return -ERANGE;
+	if (tmp < 1)
+		tmp = 1;
+	low = tmp - 1;
+
 	/*
 	 * Specification says timer usage flow is to disable timer, then
 	 * program it followed by enable. It also says Load Count is loaded
